TY  - JOUR
AU  - G Dushnitsky
TI  - Artificial intelligence and venture capital: revisiting sourcing, selection, and nuturing activities
JO  - London Business School Research Online (London Business School)
PY  - 2026
AB  - Venture capital (VC) is an information-intensive form of entrepreneurial finance conducted under extreme uncertainty. Investors must originate promising opportunities, select ventures with sparse and strategically disclosed information, and monitor and support firms after investment. Prior research documents both formal mechanisms (e.g., staged financing, syndication) and informal mechanisms built on referrals, reputation, and constant interaction. Geographic and social proximity enable these mechanisms by lowering the cost of obtaining contextual information and coordinating action. We develop a mechanism-based account of how data and Artificial Intelligence (AI) reconfigure this established model. Our framework yields propositions on geographic dispersion in sourcing, portfolio selection, and the distinct effects of AI on monitoring versus value-added support. It also identifies boundary conditions associated with institutional setting and heterogeneous AI adoption.
