TY  - JOUR
AU  - Changbong Kim
AU  - YoonWon Choi
TI  - The Effect of R&D Intensity of Deep Tech Startups on VC Investment Intention: The Mediating Effect of Perceived Follow-on Investment Risk
PY  - 2026
AB  - This study examines how the R&D intensity of deep tech startups affects the investment intention of venture capital (VC) investment managers, focusing on the mediating role of perceived follow-on investment risk and exit uncertainty and on the moderating role of long-term capital supply conditions. Drawing on the R&D financing literature and deep tech investment literature, R&D intensity is conceptualized as perceived resource-input intensity comprising three sub-dimensions: financial, temporal, and technological intensity. The mediator captures VC managers' perceptions of the likelihood of securing follow-on investors, the burden of additional financing, and exit timing uncertainty. Fund life and the practice of reserving follow-on capital are modeled as moderators, and institution type and investment stage are examined through multi-group analysis. Survey data were collected from 176 Korean VC, corporate VC, and accelerator investment managers using a 7-point Likert scale, and the model was tested using partial least squares structural equation modeling. R&D intensity significantly increased perceived follow-on investment risk, which in turn significantly reduced investment intention. Bootstrapping confirmed a significant indirect effect, whereas the direct effect of R&D intensity on investment intention was not significant, indicating indirect-only mediation. This pattern held for each sub-dimension and after controlling for respondent and institutional characteristics. Neither fund life nor follow-on reserve practice significantly weakened the negative effect of perceived risk on investment intention; however, among institutions that reserve follow-on capital in advance, the path from R&D intensity to perceived risk was significantly stronger. Multi-group analyses by institution type (VC, corporate VC, accelerator) and investment stage revealed no significant differences in path coefficients across groups. Full collinearity and marker-variable tests showed no evidence of common method bias. Theoretically, this study explains how the dual nature of R&D intensity is translated into investment intention through investors' assessment of follow-on financing risk, and clarifies that the finding is compatible with VCs' emphasis on technological capability. Practically, the findings suggest that entrepreneurship education and university-based technology venturing support should address follow-on funding roadmap design as a distinct competency, rather than treating investor persuasion as a matter of demonstrating technological merit alone.
