AU  - Youngmi Seo
AU  - Jung-Min Nam
TI  - Priority Analysis of Startup Support Infrastructure by Growth Stage for the Sustainable Growth of Startups
PY  - 2026
AB  - This study examines the relative importance and priorities of startup support infrastructure across the early, growth, and scale-up stages. Based on prior studies and expert consultation, a hierarchical structure of five main factors and 20 sub-factors was developed. An AHP survey was conducted with 20 experts with more than 10 years of startup support experience across all three growth stages, and 17 responses meeting the consistency criterion were analyzed. Follow-up interviews with three startup CEOs who had progressed through the early and growth stages and were currently in the scale-up stage were used to complement the contextual interpretation of the results. The results show that market entry and commercialization had the highest priority at the early stage, while finance and investment ranked highest at the growth and scale-up stages. At the sub-factor level, customer, market, and demand validation and MVP/productization were prioritized at the early stage; private investment/IR linkage and domestic market channels/business collaboration at the growth stage; and follow-on investment/growth finance and private investment/IR linkage at the scale-up stage. In addition, priority functions within the same support domain changed across growth stages: finance shifted from operating funds to private investment and growth finance, market support from demand validation to domestic and global expansion, talent support from problem-solving mentoring to securing key talent, and facilities/support linkage from incubation space to networks and follow-up support. These findings suggest that startup support infrastructure should be designed around changing priority functions according to firms’ commercialization progress, with resources and follow-up support linked to stage-specific growth challenges.
